A single oak twig rests in the damp mulch—thin, rigid, and forgotten. Up close, its surface tells a silent story of extraction: it is scarred by fine node lines where leaves were once attached, forced to yield every drop of photosynthetic wealth upward to nourish a canopy it will never inhabit. The twig itself receives only the leftover nutrients required to maintain its structural rigidity, kept just strong enough to hold up the heavy foliage above. It is essential to the tree's survival, yet entirely disposable. When storm winds blow, it is this thin branch that snaps first, discarded to the forest floor while the trunk remains unbothered, retaining the vast reserves of energy accumulated from the twig's lifetime of labor.

This is the precise mechanics of the modern proletariat. The contemporary worker exists as that fragile branch, bound to an vast, top-heavy corporate hierarchy. They spend the peak hours of their lives absorbing the raw pressures of production, channeling surplus value upward into the soaring canopy of executive wealth and shareholder equity. In exchange, they are granted just enough compensation to remain functional—just enough bark to stay rigid for another week of output. Should economic shifts or restructuring occur, it is never the trunk that is sacrificed; the frontline worker is snapped off without hesitation, rendered obsolete the moment their yield slows, while the institution consumes the accumulated capital of their efforts.

Yet the modern subjugation relies on a far more insidious trick than mere force: the twig is conditioned to worship the canopy. Through manufactured cultural narratives, the worker is trained to revere the very icons of their exploitation. Wealth is framed not as the result of collective labor diverted upward, but as proof of inherent moral and intellectual superiority. Millions gaze at the ultra-rich through glowing screens, adopting their aesthetics, celebrating their excesses, and defending their tax structures, fully convinced that with enough rigidity and submission, any twig might one day grow into a massive trunk. This blind devotion transforms systemic oppression into a spectacle of aspiration, keeping the exploited aligned with the interests of those who consume them.

Over time, this intense focus on height, power, and accumulated capital begins to break down, softening under the weight of its own absurd abstraction. The rigid hierarchies of wood and wealth crumble, dissolving into the quiet, humble reality of the organic world underneath.

Down near the earth, unconcerned with reaching for the sky, sits a simple head of butterhead lettuce. Its broad, delicate leaves curl loosely around one another in soft, translucent layers of pale green, held together not by rigid bark or competitive striving, but by simple moisture and soil. Water droplets pool in the gentle folds of its ruffled edges, catching the low light without demanding it. It does not store power, nor does it erect a spire to dominate the garden. It simply expands outward—tender, perishable, and completely rooted in the present moment, offering its quiet nourishment before returning effortlessly to the earth.
